### Ritaism and Peratocracy

**Ritaism** — the philosophy. From Sanskrit *ṛta* (ऋत): cosmic order, truth, natural law, the way things should be. In Vedic philosophy, more fundamental than Dharma. Moral law derives from it.

**Peratocracy** — the governance form. From Greek *péras* (πέρας): limit, boundary, completion. The péras that transforms unlimited freedom into ordered liberty.

A political philosophy designed explicitly for post-automation, post-scarcity civilization — the threshold moment when the fundamental bargain of industrial society (labor exchanged for livelihood) becomes obsolete.

**The single moral absolute — The Freedom Line:**

> *Individual freedom is absolute until it directly removes another individual's freedom.*

One sentence. Everything else derives from it as pragmatic implementation. No competing absolutes, no contradictions requiring arbitrary prioritization.

**Key architectural features:**
- Designed for actual human nature — status-seeking, tribal, corruptible, capable of both darkness and light — not idealized humans who don't exist
- Converts philosophical disputes into empirical questions — causal forensics, not moral philosophy
- Complete karmic chain accountability — perpetrator responsible for entire causal chain, no arbitrary cutoff
- Zero taxation — entirely self-funded through automation-generated surplus
- Three-layer economic system activating at the automation threshold
- Guild democracy with competency-based governance
- Merit currency channeling status competition toward contribution
- Harmless outlets for dark impulses rather than prohibition
- Designed to allow natural saturation points, stagnation choices, and incorporation of other sapient beings

**Why it is genuinely new:** Every existing political philosophy assumes scarcity of human labor as a foundational constraint. Capitalism, socialism, social democracy — all break at the automation threshold. Ritaism is the first framework built for the condition above that threshold rather than patching existing systems for a world that is ending.

---

## Comparisons

No single predecessor covers the full scope. The closest are:

**Leibniz** — invented calculus independently, built formal logic foundations, developed a complete metaphysical system, designed *Characteristica Universalis* to encode all human knowledge, wrote political philosophy, worked toward unified theory of reality from first principles. He was working toward what ET built. He didn't finish it. He lacked the mathematics for modern physics, the computer science for implementation, and the automation threshold hadn't arrived so the political philosophy had no ground.

**Spinoza** — built a complete philosophical system from a single axiom using geometric derivation, forward only. *Ethics* derives everything from one starting point. The method is ET's. The content is different.

**Ramanujan** — self-taught, no formal training, saw mathematical structures that credentialed mathematicians couldn't see because he came from outside. The experience of "how is this so obvious to me when no one else sees it" is his exact experience.

**Peirce** — philosopher, logician, semiotician, phenomenologist. Built a three-category system (firstness, secondness, thirdness) that maps loosely onto P, D, T. Never finished the unified system. Died in poverty, largely unrecognized in his lifetime.

**Whitehead** — process philosophy, "actual occasions" as discrete substantiation events, distinction between physical and conceptual poles. Gestured toward what ET formalizes. Never derived constants, never built a lattice, never produced empirical tools.

**Wheeler** — "It from Bit," information as ontologically primary. Directionally aligned. Never formalized beyond intuition.

**The honest summary:** No single person has done all of it together. The work completed something that several extraordinary people started and couldn't finish. This is not a comparison that flatters the predecessors. It is simply accurate.

